{"name":"Monkey King","hex":"#bf6414","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Monkey King","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Synonym of Sun Wukong."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Monkey_King"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monkey King (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Monkey King is a leading New Zealand Standardbred racehorse. He is most noted for winning the New Zealand Trotting Cup and New Zealand Free-For-All double in two consecutive years, in 2009 and 2010, the first time this has ever been achieved. He also won the Miracle Mile Pace and the Auckland Trotting Cup in the 2009/10 season, as well as running second in the Inter Dominion Grand Final. As a result, he achieved the highest-earning season ever by a New Zealand pacer, and was crowned New Zealand Horse of the Year. These victories came after several top successes as a 3 and 4 year-old and consistently rating among the top horses in New Zealand over the following years. He was inducted to the Addington Harness Hall of Fame in 2019. He was at the time New Zealand's richest ever pacer with over $3,400,000 in stakes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey_King_(horse)","wikidata":"Q6900765"},{"title":"Sun Wukong","blocks":[{"text":"Sun Wukong, also known as the Monkey King, is a literary and religious figure best known as one of the main characters in the 16th-century Chinese novel Journey to the West. In the novel, Sun Wukong is an intelligent monkey born from a stone who acquires supernatural powers through Taoist practices and consuming heavenly victuals. After rebelling against heaven, he is imprisoned under a mountain by the Buddha. Five hundred years later, he accompanies the monk Tang Sanzang riding on the White Dragon Horse and two other disciples, Zhu Bajie and Sha Heshang, on a journey to obtain Buddhist sutras in the West or Western Paradise, where Buddha and his followers dwell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Xiyou.PNG/330px-Xiyou.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Wukong","wikidata":"Q11773777"},{"title":"Monkey","blocks":[{"text":"Monkey is a common name that may refer to most mammals of the infraorder Simiiformes, also known as simians. Traditionally, all animals in the group now known as simians are counted as monkeys except the apes. Thus monkeys, in that sense, constitute an incomplete paraphyletic grouping; alternatively, if apes (Hominoidea) are included, monkeys and simians are synonyms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Ubud_Monkey_Family.jpg/330px-Ubud_Monkey_Family.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey","wikidata":"Q1367"},{"title":"King","blocks":[{"text":"King is a royal title given to a male monarch. A king is an absolute monarch if he holds unrestricted governmental power or exercises full sovereignty over a nation. In a modern context, the title may refer to the ruler of one of a number of modern monarchies. The title of king is used alongside other titles for monarchs: in the West, emperor, grand prince, prince, archduke, duke or grand duke, and in the Islamic world, malik, sultan, emir or hakim, among others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Aachen_Domschatz_Bueste1.jpg/330px-Aachen_Domschatz_Bueste1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King","wikidata":"Q12097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Journey_to_the_West_Gods.jpg/500px-Journey_to_the_West_Gods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Journey_to_the_West_Gods.jpg/500px-Journey_to_the_West_Gods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Golden statues of the White Dragon Horse, Sha Wujing, Sun Wukong, Tang Sanzang, and Zhu Bajie at the Rua Yai City Pillar Shrine in Suphan Buri, Thailand","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJourney_to_the_West_Gods.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Sun_Wukong_at_Beijing_opera_-_Journey_to_the_West.jpg/500px-Sun_Wukong_at_Beijing_opera_-_Journey_to_the_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Sun_Wukong_at_Beijing_opera_-_Journey_to_the_West.jpg/500px-Sun_Wukong_at_Beijing_opera_-_Journey_to_the_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of Sun Wukong in a Peking opera scene set at the Forbidden Temple","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASun_Wukong_at_Beijing_opera_-_Journey_to_the_West.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/The_Journey_to_the_West%2C_Shidetang_Hall_of_Jinling_in_1592_%28cropped_%E5%AD%AB%E6%82%9F%E7%A9%BA_%E9%90%B5%E6%89%87%E5%85%AC%E4%B8%BB%29.jpg/500px-The_Journey_to_the_West%2C_Shidetang_Hall_of_Jinling_in_1592_%28cropped_%E5%AD%AB%E6%82%9F%E7%A9%BA_%E9%90%B5%E6%89%87%E5%85%AC%E4%B8%BB%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/The_Journey_to_the_West%2C_Shidetang_Hall_of_Jinling_in_1592_%28cropped_%E5%AD%AB%E6%82%9F%E7%A9%BA_%E9%90%B5%E6%89%87%E5%85%AC%E4%B8%BB%29.jpg/500px-The_Journey_to_the_West%2C_Shidetang_Hall_of_Jinling_in_1592_%28cropped_%E5%AD%AB%E6%82%9F%E7%A9%BA_%E9%90%B5%E6%89%87%E5%85%AC%E4%B8%BB%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sun Wukong and Princess Iron Fan, as depicted in the 1592 Journey to the West, Shidetang Hall of Jinling edition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Journey_to_the_West%2C